WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:03.800
Andrew Horng, Writer/Director:

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.720
Nowadays, there are competitions out there
that challenge you to make a short film over

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.720
the course of just one weekend.&nbsp;&nbsp;They’re
a really fun way to gain experience, network,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.040
and create a film quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;Very quickly.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.439
Last year, I entered my first “timed”
competition, and the short film I wrote placed

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:26.080
1st out of 58 total films.&nbsp;&nbsp;I met Marcia
Gay Harden, I got into Variety, and I was

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.899
even invited by my team to compete for a second
and third time, writing films that ranked

00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:35.460
among the very best.&nbsp;&nbsp;Today, I’m gonna
teach you what I’ve learned from my experiences

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:41.480
to bypass the competition and get you into
the cream of the crop.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.760
KNOWING YOUR GENRE

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.700
The first thing you gotta know is… for these
challenges, the shorts are due on Sunday,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.539
which means you really only have Friday to
work on the story and deliver a script.&nbsp;&nbsp;In

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:58.449
addition to that, the competition wants to
make sure that you don’t start any earlier,

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:04.480
so they give you a specific list of requirements
on Friday that must be present in your film.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.980
My first piece of advice is to split these
requirements up.&nbsp;&nbsp;Ignore minor requirements

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:14.750
like props or dialogue, and start with the
one that matters the most: genre.&nbsp;&nbsp;Genre

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.870
is going to be your compass, pointing your
story in the right direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;You need

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:24.670
to deconstruct that genre, and figure out
what that genre is really all about on a sub-atomic

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:25.790
level.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:31.520
For example, we were given the fantasy genre
for our second film “Crystal’s Ball.”&nbsp;&nbsp;After

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.450
researching examples online, I noticed that
many fantasy stories follow a similar structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;A

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:43.130
character has some sort of pain and enters
a sort of fantasy land to escape that pain.&nbsp;&nbsp;But

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:48.530
it’s not all peaches and creme there, and
the character eventually returns to the real

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:53.990
world with a new outlook from the entire experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;And
that’s what we ended up doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;We told

00:01:53.990 --> 00:02:00.860
a story about a grieving daughter that uses
a crystal ball to reunite with her dead father.&nbsp;&nbsp;But

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.710
their reunion is not all peaches and creme.&nbsp;&nbsp;And
near the end of the film, she leaves and is

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.700
a tad different from where she started.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.810
ACTORS &amp; LOCATIONS

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:16.430
After breaking down your genre into its most
basic form, you should then consider the two

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:21.750
big assets you’ll be working with: actors
and location.&nbsp;&nbsp;Because time was of the essence

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:27.170
for our films, both the actors and the locations
were already set each time before the story

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:32.300
was even conceived.&nbsp;&nbsp;But that’s actually
okay since those two factors, along with an

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:37.220
understanding of the genre, are a great launching
point for brainstorming.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:42.990
My first suggestion for generating ideas is
to zero in on conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;Conflict is the

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:48.410
life blood of any story, even a short one
told in just a few minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;What might

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:53.040
help you get in this conflict mindset is to
just casually think about what a potential

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.480
problem could be when you factor in both your
actors and your location.&nbsp;&nbsp;And then after

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.900
that, a potential solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;When you’ve
come up with some possibilities, I’d suggest

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.790
you start your story with the problem that
excites you the most.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.870
That being said, my second suggestion during
this stage is to keep it simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;You don’t

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.360
have hours to tell your story, only minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;And
you don’t have much time to write it out

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:21.100
either.&nbsp;&nbsp;You can be more detail-oriented
later in the process, but it probably won’t

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.970
do you any favors to do so at the very start.&nbsp;&nbsp;What
may help with simplicity is to think of your

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:33.120
story in threes.&nbsp;&nbsp;Take a step back, look
at the big picture, and try to describe your

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.260
story’s beginning, middle, and end in the
most simplistic way.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.810
For example, before writing our first film
“Check Mate” we had a house as our location

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.530
and four actors cast already.&nbsp;&nbsp;So, zeroing
in on the conflict, the problem that was the

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:52.870
most exciting was about the house having a
vacancy, and two of our actors fighting over

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:58.080
it.&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, the other two actors became
the ones who already lived there.&nbsp;&nbsp;So then,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.590
expanding that premise into threes… the
two actors meet at the house in the beginning

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:08.420
of the story… they fight over the vacancy
in the middle… and then they quickly become

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.180
friends and decide to room together by the
end.&nbsp;&nbsp;They meet.&nbsp;&nbsp;They fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;They

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.709
become friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;Simple.

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:19.569
TREATMENT STAGE

00:04:19.569 --> 00:04:24.030
When you’ve figured out the simple beginning,
middle, and end of your story, you could just

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:30.289
jump into writing the first draft of the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;Or,
what I recommend, is to take another writing

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:36.590
step in between and write out a brief synopsis
of your story in just a couple of paragraphs.&nbsp;&nbsp;By

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:42.060
doing so, it gives you another opportunity
to rewrite your story in a new format and

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.349
do so quickly.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:48.759
Also, a brief synopsis is going to be a tad
more detailed, so here you’re going to want

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:53.639
to add in the “lesser” requirements from
the competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;Requirements like what

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:59.009
props to put in or what dialogue to say will
be easier to plug into your story, now that

00:04:59.009 --> 00:05:02.509
you already have the “backbone” for your
story figured out.

00:05:02.509 --> 00:05:06.919
If you’re with another writer or the director
at this stage, this is your chance to pitch

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:12.430
them with your synopsis and get them on board
with your story before you flesh it out in

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:18.669
script format.&nbsp;&nbsp;Especially if you or someone
else is not quite sold on the idea yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;Here,

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:23.419
you can get feedback regarding what you need
to improve on, or you can get confidence with

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:25.970
what’s working with your story.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.580
WRITE IT QUICKLY

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.430
With all that very loose planning done, and
all your thinking power focused on what assets

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.689
you have and what requirements you need to
check off, you can now focus on writing your

00:05:39.689 --> 00:05:44.620
story out in script format.&nbsp;&nbsp;I use Final
Draft, but really any screenwriting software

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.440
out there should help you create your final
version as quickly as possible.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.129
For this last step, I’d suggest pulling
up your synopsis, and then quickly writing

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:59.250
out a script version of it that you’ll probably
think is bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;Also, I would restrain yourself

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:04.189
from making any edits to it until you’ve
finished writing this bad draft.&nbsp;&nbsp;But keep

00:06:04.189 --> 00:06:08.090
in mind, even though you’ve already done
a bunch of planning, this is still your very

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:13.120
first draft of the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;It’s supposed
to be rough and unpolished, so don’t beat

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.250
yourself up over the quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;Just focus
on converting your synopsis into a bad draft

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:23.400
first, and then (after you’re done with
that step) look at it with a critical eye

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.440
and make some edits for your final draft.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.479
EASTERSEALS DISABILITY FILM CHALLENGE

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:36.409
So my first and third films I did for competition
were for the Easterseals Disability Film Challenge,

00:06:36.409 --> 00:06:42.479
which was amazing to be a part of.&nbsp;&nbsp;Disability
is very much underrepresented and misrepresented

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:47.199
in the media, and being a part of the challenge
helped to move the needle when it came to

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:48.199
that.

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:52.729
The competition welcomes all filmmakers who
are down with that cause and down to make

00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:58.080
something really quick.&nbsp;&nbsp;So now that I’ve
given you some tips on how to write for a

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.240
timed competition, I hope you’ll consider
joining that one and making an impact as well.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.939
If you want to check it out, or any of the
films, please see the links in the description

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:10.529
below.&nbsp;&nbsp;Good luck, and I’ll see you next
time on Film Courage.

